Yes, I realize that this is just adding one more message to an already long
thread, but having lived in the Orient for 7+ years, I figured I'd add my
two cents in.  :-)

In article <1991Oct25.161910.2442@cunixf.cc.columbia.edu> jgl7@cunixa.cc.columbia.edu (Andrew T. Kim (Taeyon Kim)) writes:
> ... The dance features Bali dancers who are "in trance" lunging knives
>called "Krisknife" at themselves.  I was just wondering whether the
>crysknife in nethack has its origin from Bali's "Krisknife" or from some
>Hindu culture.

The "kris" or, as it is modernly often called, the "kris-knife" (an English
perversion of the name - no, not British - the language) is a very common
type of knife in the Orient, from China to the Philippines to Indonesia,
etc.  It is a wavy-bladed knife, that can be sized from a dagger (6" blade
or so) up to about a short sword (17" blade or so).  The same type of blade
is also often used in Muslim/Middle-Eastern cultures, although there it is
most often called a "tulwar."

It is known that Frank Herbert, when he wrote his book "Dune," took _many_
of his terms from foreign cultures and religions, relying heavily on
the Islamic faith, and mixing in some Catholic beliefs.  It is possible 
that the "crysknife" of Dune fame (a sacred and holy blade to the Fremen of
Arrakis, or Dune) had its name taken from the wavy bladed knifes used in
the middle-east and the orient.

As it stands in Dune, however, the "crysknife" is a blade that is fashioned
from the tooth of a sandworm.  It is a holy blade, and is fashioned for a
single individual.  If it is away from the presence of that individual for
more than a few days, it will become brittle and disintegrate.  Also, if it
is drawn, it must draw blood before being sheathed again.

Anyways...  Hope this was interesting for those of you who wanted to know.

